   "date": "2017-05-18T14:13:04Z",
   "subject": "Determine modules in 3-dimensional data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br>Dear all,<br><br>I&#39;m working on a set of skulls and I wish to find out to each modules my landmarks should be assigned and also how many modules exist. I&#39;ve searched in the Geomorph 3.0 files but all methods I find require a modules known <i>a priori</i>, which allow me to test hypothesis but not to find out which landmarks are more correlated to each other. I found the function <div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">define</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">modules</span></div></code></div>the closest to what I wish to do, but still it requires <i>a priori</i> known modules. My question is: is there any way to determine modules with Geomorph?<br><br>I checked on the methodology of  Goswami, A. (2006), which appears to be the most straightforward way to determine modules.However, one of the products of gpagen is the vaeiance-covariance matrix of coordinates. Is there a way to obtain the variance-covariance matrix of landmarks, do that in order to then be able to do something like this (see below), to find the most correlated landmarks?<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">gpa </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> gpagen</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Landma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">print</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">progress </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">d </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dist</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">gpa$points</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">VCV</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> method </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;euclidean&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>fit</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">hclust</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">d</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">method</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;ward.D&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><br>Thank you, in advance, for your attention.<br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
